Jonathan Shrier
Acting Representative of the United States on the Economic and Social Council of the United Nations
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Mr. President, the United States has an objection. We would like to thank the Executive Board for this opportunity to speak to the United States’ concerns regarding the Country Program Document (CPD) for China and to request a vote on the decision to approve this CPD.
The United States will not support initiatives that do not align with U.S. priorities. As an Executive Board member, the United States takes its oversight responsibility over agency operations and how they allocate their resources seriously, including for the UNFPA Country Program Document for China for 2026-2030.
China’s coercive family planning policies are antithetical to America’s promotion of individual liberty.
The United States remains prohibited from funding any organization that supports or participates in the management of a program of coercive abortion or involuntary sterilization. The United States stands for the rights of the individual – rights that the Chinese government utterly disregards.
The UNFPA’s previous legitimization of this effort, and continued partnership with a Chinese government entity that implements abhorrent population control policies which include coercive elements, is unacceptable. By letting China draw on UNFPA resources, we risk subsidizing China’s own geopolitical agenda under a multilateral umbrella.
The UN was formed to support the vulnerable, not underwrite the geopolitical ambitions of a major power. We regret this item is even up for review, which is why we will be voting against this Country Program Document.
For decades, the United States has called on UNFPA to end its partnership with the Chinese government agency responsible for China’s population control. Millions of families have suffered under China’s destructive approach to population. Generations of women continue to suffer the harm of this excruciating legacy which included barbaric forced abortions and involuntary sterilization. Yet again, we urge the Executive Board to end UNFPA’s complicity.
The United States also strongly objects to the UN’s promotion of any Member State’s individual development policy, including the Global Development Initiative.
Mr. President, thank you for the opportunity to request a vote and deliver this explanation of vote.
