New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Mr. President, the United States has an objection and wishes to request a vote on the decision to adopt a Country Program Document for China.
The United States will not support initiatives that do not align with U.S. priorities. As an Executive Board member, the United States takes its oversight responsibilities over agency operations and how they allocate their resources seriously, including for the UNDP Country Program Document for China for 2026-2030.
China is the world’s second largest economy. A country that can fund its own space program and build aircraft carriers should fund its own development. Every UNDP dollar spent in China is one less for countries that truly depend on that funding. Letting China draw on UNDP resources risks subsidizing one country’s geopolitical agenda under a multilateral umbrella. The UN was formed to support the vulnerable, not underwrite geopolitical ambitions of a major power. This is why we are requesting a vote and voting against this Country Program Document. We know that China is watching every vote, and many countries cannot take a stance on this issue fearing aggressive retaliation. We are not afraid to stand up, and we are not afraid to state that scarce resources should not be diverted to Member States that can manage their own development priorities.
Moving forward, we urge the Executive Board to ensure justifiable criteria – not political self-identification – serve as the basis for resource allocation by UNDP.
The United States also strongly objects to the UN’s promotion of any Member State’s foreign or development policy, including the Global Development Initiative.
Mr. President, thank you for the opportunity to request a vote and deliver this explanation of vote.
