Jonathan Shrier
Acting Representative of the United States on the Economic and Social Council of the United Nations
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. Chair.
In so many ways, we are living in a very different world today from the world we knew in January. The foreign assistance landscape has been remade as members focus on national needs and priorities and the UN turns to reform.
And yet, the world is very much the same. Conflict, disease, malnutrition, and war threaten the lives of far too many. How do each of us deal with all these challenges, either directly or indirectly?
This is where UNOPS comes in. UNOPS is uniquely placed to catalyze and support the ethos of partnership the world needs today due to its partnership mandate, infrastructure expertise, and track record.
But as an organization, UNOPS needs to walk the talk, including by putting agency partnerships, and not profit margins, at the heart of its business model. UNOPS can and should be the fulcrum of collective action. UNOPS must focus on its core mandate and comparative advantage, avoid programmatic work, and avoid distracting narratives that fall outside that mission – like the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development and ambiguous gender language.
UNOPS also needs to make management’s relationship with staff a truly accountable, communicative, and mutually beneficial partnership. The terrific talent in this organization needs to feel valued, safe, and have a firm understanding of where each member fits in the organization’s mission, both present and future.
Partnership also means leveraging opportunities, like global shared services and common back offices, to join collectively with other entities to address challenges in the field. One such opportunity is to join the Quantum Enterprise Risk Management (ERM) framework, enabling country teams to efficiently and cost-effectively manage important but costly administrative functions such as payroll. We join other members in urging UNOPS to take that step.
In the field, UNOPS staff have come through time and time again to save lives, clear the rubble, and rebuild communities. It is time for management to come through and live the partnership values needed for these times and worthy of their efforts.
Thank you.
