Jonathan Shrier
Acting Representative of the United States on the Economic and Social Council of the United Nations
New York, New York
08/26/2025
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President and thank you, Acting Executive Director Keita for your statement and recognition that the agency’s mandate must remain in areas of clear consensus. The United States pursues shared objectives for improved health for women and girls through the Geneva Consensus Declaration.
As the UN system must recognize the strengths, expertise, and sovereignty of its respective members, we recognize technical partnerships with member states who drive forward quality efforts on key issues like improving demographic data, understanding low fertility as well as infertility, and recognizing the opportunities within our rapidly aging world. However, all too frequently, the United States is reminded of the UN’s blinding biases. The United States will always remain committed to combatting the UN’s anti-Israel bias.
The appointment of a new Executive Director for UNFPA puts its future at a crossroads. The Secretary General’s selection should unify member states. It is paramount that the consequential leadership decision respect and advance the integrity of UNFPA’s mandate.
Mr President, we further underscore that that United States has always urged UNFPA to condemn coercive population efforts and specifically end its partnership with the Chinese government agency responsible for cruelly implementing such policies. Women in China still suffer from the impacts of complicit Chinese officials who supported campaigns of forced sterilization and forced abortion. This legacy which undermines UNFPA’s credibility should not mar its future.
We again reiterate that member states should unequivocally not subsidize UNFPA’s technical assistance for a country with the world’s second largest economy in the world. We view any allocation of resources to China as a continued failing of the Board’s fiduciary responsibility.
Thank you.
