Statement at the Interactive Dialogue with UNDP Acting Administrator Haoliang Xu, 2025 Second Regular Session of the UNDP/UNOPS/UNFPA Executive Board
Jonathan Shrier
Acting Representative of the United States on the Economic and Social Council of the United Nations
New York, New York
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President. In this time of change and reform, the United States expects UNDP to adapt to the new funding landscape and to achieve greater efficiency by eliminating duplication, consolidating functions, and returning to core mandates. This also means not being sidetracked by narratives that fall outside UNDP’s core mission – or reporting to soft global governance frameworks like the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development.
Another way UNDP – and all UN funds and programs – can adjust to the changing fiscal environment is by continuing to improve private sector collaboration and considering innovative financing. They must also prioritize allocating funding to countries with the greatest needs.
This is why we object to resources being diverted to China and are dismayed that the world’s second-largest economy, nay the United Nations’ second-largest assessed donor is being prioritized over true developing countries. A country that can fund its own space program should not poach limited resources from countries that need clean water and basic infrastructure.
It is unacceptable that the Country Program Document for China does not include an exit strategy and promotes the Global Development Initiative, a vehicle of China’s foreign policy that imposes one country’s strategic ambition onto others, and binds recipients to China’s economic orbit at the cost of their freedom of choice. The farce of one of the most technologically advanced countries on earth seeking developing country status must end.
Despite China’s claims of championing developing countries, this is yet another instance of China only championing its own interests. As UNDP moves forward with the next strategic plan and budget, it must not promote a particular country’s agenda, national development initiatives, or priorities.
In an era of diminishing financial resources, it is essential that UNDP focus on its core purpose, uses accurate methodologies, and promotes exact definitions. In this same light, the United States expects UNDP to ensure that its programming, strategies, and documents discontinue the use of “gender” in favor of specific language on “women, men, girls, and boys.”
We stress that UNDP should focus on specific development objectives, such as eradicating poverty, addressing corruption, improving health, promoting food security, and improving education, among others.
UNDP must focus on effective delivery where it matters most – at the country level. We encourage UNDP, and all other Funds and Programs, to remain focused on its founding principles and not be distracted by narratives that fall outside its core mission.
We also look forward to a new administrator being appointed as soon as possible to lead the agency through this pivotal time for the UN and the agency.
Thank you.
